IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
CASE NO: Writ Petition (Civil) No. 1474 of 1973
CITATION: (1973) 4 SCC 225
YEAR: 1973

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Judges: S.M. Sikri, J.M. Shelat, K.S. Hegde, A.N. Grover, et al. (13-Judge Bench)

PARTIES:
Kesavananda Bharati vs State of Kerala

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Constitution of India

KEY PROVISIONS & SECTIONS:
Article 368, Article 13, Article 31C

LEGAL KEYWORDS & TOPICS:
basic structure doctrine, amendment limits, judicial review, sovereignty

================================================================================
                    JUDGMENT SUMMARY & RATIO DECIDENDI
================================================================================

Thirteen-judge bench formulated the Basic Structure Doctrine, establishing that Parliament's constituent power to amend the Constitution cannot alter or destroy its basic features.

1. ISSUES CONSIDERED:
   The court addressed questions of legal interpretation under Article 368, Article 13, Article 31C.

2. ANALYSIS & PRECEDENT CONTEXT:
   Having examined the statutory scheme, fundamental principles of jurisprudence,
   and constitutional safeguards, the Court reiterated the necessity of strict
   compliance with statutory procedure and evidentiary standards.

3. HELD & BINDING RATIO:
   The principle formulated in this judgment constitutes binding judicial precedent
   within its territorial and appellate jurisdiction.

